window.docContents[54].resolve({"/UsingLean/#Logical-Foundations--UsingLean___-Using-the-Full-Power-of-a-Proof-Assistant--More-Powerful-Natural-Numbers":{"contents":"\n\nWhereas we performed manual rewrite steps on our custom Nats ...\n\nsection OldNats\nopen NatPlayground.Nat\nexample : (two * two : NatPlayground.Nat) = four := by\n  rewrite [two_eq_succ_one, one_eq_succ_zero]\n  rewrite [mul_succ, mul_succ, mul_zero]\n  rewrite [add_succ, add_succ, add_zero]\n  rewrite [add_succ, add_succ, add_zero]\n  rfl\n\n\n\n\n... we can simplify Lean's built-in Nats automatically.\n\nend OldNats\n-- Now, we are using Lean's built-in natural numbers.\nexample : (2 * 2 : Nat) = 4 := by rfl\n\n\n\n\nDoing so is very helpful for large numbers — we would not want to write out\nthe hundreds or thousands of rewrite steps needed for proving examples like the\nfollowing!\n\nexample : (2 * 3 + 4 * 5 : Nat) * 6 = 156 := by rfl\n\n\nOf course, rfl still can't close goals where the values of the terms are unknown.\n\nexample (n m : Nat) (h : n = m) : n = m := by\n  -- `rfl` will not work here!\n  -- First rewrite the goal with `h`; then the two sides are identical.\n  rw [h]\n\n\nFrom now on we will use the built-in Nat type.\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009UsingLean: Using the Full Power of a Proof Assistant","header":"4.1. More Powerful Natural Numbers","id":"/UsingLean/#Logical-Foundations--UsingLean___-Using-the-Full-Power-of-a-Proof-Assistant--More-Powerful-Natural-Numbers"}});